[{"id":66660,"sender_id":166,"sender_full_name":"Marc Hoyois","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/cd33109a9477ad199b524b54663fb674?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Comment.20about.20nlab.20entry.20on.20motivic.20homotopy.20theory/near/66660\">#3</a> I only just found this thread. This was indeed inaccurate, so I removed it. There is only a non-genuine Galois-equivariant realization, and the target is a nonstandard category of pro-spectra where not only S^1 but also its Tate twist (= the etale homotopy type of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf G_m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8361em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">G</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">m</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>) are inverted.\nI believe that a genuine Galois-equivariant realization exists iff <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span> is a real closed field. There are no references for any of these claims, however.</p>","timestamp":1520277328,"subject":"Comment about nlab entry on motivic homotopy theory","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":66664,"sender_id":237,"sender_full_name":"Richard Williamson","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b90dbe6063b47a44f7308efebb6ade2b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Comment.20about.20nlab.20entry.20on.20motivic.20homotopy.20theory/near/66664\">#4</a> Hi Marc, whilst you’re here, I don’t suppose you’d have any thoughts about my question <a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Tate.20twists.20and.20twisted.20cohomology/with/66265\">here</a>? (And feel free to improve on what I wrote at <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Tate%20twist\">Tate twist</a> :-))</p>","timestamp":1520278814,"subject":"Comment about nlab entry on motivic homotopy theory","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]}]